# Compiled test binaries, which `go test -c` leaves behind.
*.test

# Profiling output.
*.out
*.prof

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store

# Differential-test corpora. These are third-party material — production
# stylesheets and rule sets, plus the Saxon output they are diffed against —
# so they are not redistributed here. The tests that use them skip cleanly
# when the directory is absent; see "Testing" in the README for how to point
# the suite at your own corpus.
# Both spellings on purpose. "/testdata/" matches a directory and nothing
# else, so a *symlink* named testdata is not ignored by it — which is how a
# worktree's symlink to the real corpora was once committed, and then written
# over the real directory when that commit was reverted. "/testdata" matches
# whatever the name refers to.
/testdata
/testdata/

# Local tooling, not part of the library.
#
# .githooks holds the pre-commit hook that refuses a commit by anyone but the
# repository owner. It is deliberately not distributed: core.hooksPath is a
# local setting, a clone would not run it without being told to, and shipping
# a hook that silently does nothing is worse than shipping none.
/.githooks/

# CLAUDE.md and .claude/ are working notes for an assistant, not documentation
# of the library. Everything they say that a reader needs is in docs/.
/CLAUDE.md
/.claude/

# Audit reports from external reviewers. Local working material: the
# findings that survive review land in CHANGELOG.md and the docs, and the
# reports themselves are not redistributed here.
/docs/audits/

# `go build ./tests/xsdsuite` and `go run` leave the driver binary at the repo
# root, where it is 11 MB and has twice been staged by accident. The package is
# a main, so it has no *.test name for the rule above to catch.
/xsdsuite

# The provenance of the last gate run: Go version, repo commit, GOOS/GOARCH,
# UTC time, and the revision of every suite that was on disk. Written by
# tests/check.sh on every run, so committing it would put a diff in the tree
# every time anyone ran the gate. It is not committed because what it proves is
# the MEASUREMENT, not the commit: it is useful attached to the figure it
# explains -- a CI artifact, or pasted beside a quoted number -- and a copy in
# git would only ever say what the last committer happened to run.
/tests/last-run.txt
